A||||5||fiero56||Running way too rich||||5||
Z||000000||fiero56||03-16-2001||11:38 AM||||'88 v-6 5-speed<P><BR>The injectors in my Fiero were leaking terribly, and had 170K miles on them. I shipped them out, had them rebuilt, and they arrived yesterday. **The stupid things I do, now**: I decided to clean up the middle intake manifold while it was still bolted to the engine with a can of carb cleaner. Before I realized it, I had sprayed the entire can(it was full)down the intake. I put the engine back together, took me a few hours to finally get it to fire up, but it did. Blowing out billows of violent black smoke until all that cleaner worked its way out(2-3 minutes). <BR>The engine doesn't run right. It is running VERY rich, and throwing code 45 at me. I replaced the O2 sensor this morning, thinking the cleaner killed it, also reset the ECM. Didn't fix it. Still throws code 45,and the exhaust smells heavily of gas. <P>No vac leaks. I am almost positive I wired each injector with its corresponding electrical plug. It idles smoothly, for about a minute, then drops, sets the code, then idles smoothly again. Any suggestions? Did I kill a differnt sensor with the cleaner?<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 03-16-2001).]||||reg||5
Z||000001||Oreif||03-16-2001||11:59 AM||||Check the catalytic converter. If it is starting to clog the car will run rich. Also some cleaners and raw gas will damage the O2 sensor.||||reg||1
Z||000002||fiero56||03-16-2001||12:29 PM||||I replaced O2 after I cleared out the cleaner.<P>Side note: the exhaust, EGR, and related components all seem much hotter than normal. Not sure if this is indicative of rich mixture or more cloesely related to CAT clogging. The car was running fine when I decided to pull the injectors.<P>It seems quieter, feels a little less rev happy(but will still run up to 5,000rpm at WOT with no problems) and at WOT has the sound a flooded engine has meaning the exhaust note is a little different. Plus, when stopped at a light or similar with the window down, I can smell my own exhaust, and it smells rich.||||reg||1
Z||000003||fiero56||03-16-2001||02:16 PM||||I took the plenum off again(that makes 3 times after I had re-assembled the engine), and found that the vacuum hose that connects to the fuel rail had pulled away from its boot. So when I looked under the plenum, it appeard to be connected, but in fact wasn't. This fixed my problem. Bottom end power is restored very nicely. More than before I had the injectors rebuilt. Was worth the money, even if the only thing I notice is the engine not flooding once I shut it off.<P>I had Marren Motorsports perform the work: <A HREF="http://www.injector.com/injectorservice.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.injector.com/injectorservice.htm</A> <P>They even sent a computer print-out of before and after flow rates and such. Did it bring back lots of lost power? No. Not a very noticeable increase in power, but that wasn't my reason for sending them in. It was so I could have a smooth running engine again, with no surging at steady throttle, and it fixed it too.||||reg||1
Z||000004||My7Fieros||03-16-2001||02:30 PM||||Hmmmm...interesting. I may have to do that.<BR>Some questions....<BR>Are you satisfied w/ their work?<BR>How long did it take?<BR>How much did it cost?<BR>M7F<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://tgowens.com/My7Fieros_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||fiero56||03-16-2001||09:25 PM||||I am very satisfied with their work. I talked to Tim, the owner, and he seems very knowledgeable. <P>The injectors arrived at their shop on a Thursday afternoon, and Marren shipped them back out to me on the following Tuesday(this was all by using the tracking # UPS gave me). <P>My problem was my injectors would never fully close, and would flood the engine with leftover fuel pressure once I shut it off. That, and the engine would surge under constant load due to some injectors pumping out more gas than others. The "differential range" was a little more than 12% between the highest and lowest flowing injectors(47.2-42.1cc's/min @ 43psi and 6ms pulse). Spray patterns were terrible on 4 of the 6, and all 6 tested dirty.<P>Several of the injectors had broken plastic heads, and were covered in varnish and gum . Marren rebuilt the injectors with all new pieces(pretty yellow pieces too), replaced all o-rings and seals, performed a resistance test on the injector windings, and balanced them so they are within a 2% margin of each other(45.8-44.8 cc's/min). Spray pattern is good, and all tested clean afterwards. <P>The fuel system now holds a pressure after I shut down the engine(engine no longer floods when turned off), the surging is gone, the engine runs very smoothly now, and there is a noticeable restoration of power in the lower rpm range. Top end feels about the same, maybe a little more power.<P>Total bill was around $150 including shipping charges. I figured it was worth it, since I can throw them on the 2.8 I am rebuilding when it is finished.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 03-16-2001).]||||reg||1
